- How would you adapt this for arbitrary word size or signed integers?
- What are the performance implications on different architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to count 1-runs in a 64-bit word using bit-twiddling with no loops.

## Key Concepts
- Start mask n & ~(n << 1) identifies 1-run starts
- Parallel popcount technique to count bits
- Edge cases: all zeros, runs spanning MSB

## Code Example
```cpp
uint64_t countOneRuns64(uint64_t n){
  uint64_t starts = n & ~(n << 1);
  uint64_t x = starts;
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 128-bit operands?
- Compare this approach to a loop-based scan in terms of latency and pipeline efficiency.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reduce a 128-bit value to a single parity bit using only bitwise ops, no branching.

## Key Concepts

- XOR-based parity reduction
- Cross word reduction
- Constant-time bit twiddling

## Code Example

```c
uint8_t parity128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo){
  uint64_t x = hi ^ lo;
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  x ^= x >> 2;
  x ^= x >> 1;
  return (uint8_t)(x & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for streaming 64-bit chunks?
- Could you optimize for a specific ISA (eg, AVX2) for parity reduction?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about cross-boundary 128-bit rotations without a native 128-bit type. Exercises correct handling of edge cases like r=0, r=64, and r=127, plus understanding how two 64-bit halves interact during rotation.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit numbers split into hi/lo halves
- Boundary-safe shifts across 64-bit parts
- Distinguishing r<64 vs r>=64 paths and their swap behavior

## Code Example

```c
uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned r) {
  r &= 127;
  if (r == 0) return x;
  if (r < 64) {
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi << r) | (x.lo >> (64 - r));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo << r) | (x.hi >> (64 - r));
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  } else {
    unsigned t = r - 64;
    uint64_t hi = (x.lo << t) | (x.hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo >> (64 - t)) | (x.hi << t);
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement rotateRight128? 
- How do you unit-test edge cases (0, 64, 127) efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
Interleaving bits to form Morton codes is common in spatial indexing and GPU data layouts. The solution enforces branchless bit spreading using fixed masks, ensuring deterministic performance.

## Key Concepts
- Morton order (Z-order) bit interleaving
- Fixed-mask bit spreading (part1by1)
- Branchless implementation

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline uint64_t part1by1(uint32_t n) {
  uint64_t v = n;
  v = (v | (v << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  v = (v | (v << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  v = (v | (v << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  v = (v | (v << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  v = (v | (v << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  return v;
}

uint64_t interleave32(uint32_t x, uint32_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 64-bit inputs producing a 128-bit result?
- How does this interact with SIMD paths for batched data?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply SWAR-style bit counting across a 128-bit structure without branches, using two independent 64-bit halves. Also checks portability when no native 128-bit constant exists.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR popcount on 64-bit chunks
- 128-bit represented as { hi, lo }
- Edge-case handling for 0 and all-ones

## Code Example

```c
uint8_t popcount128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo;
  lo = lo - ((lo >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  lo = (lo & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((lo >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  lo = (lo + (lo >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  lo = lo + (lo >> 8);
  lo = lo + (lo >> 16);
  lo = lo + (lo >> 32);

  uint64_t hi = x.hi;
  hi = hi - ((hi >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  hi = (hi & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((hi >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  hi = (hi + (hi >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  hi = hi + (hi >> 8);
  hi = hi + (hi >> 16);
  hi = hi + (hi >> 32);

  uint8_t cl = lo & 0x7F;
  uint8_t ch = hi & 0x7F;
  return cl + ch;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for wider word sizes (e.g., 256-bit)?
- Could compiler intrinsics offer portable fallbacks here?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to perform endianness reversal across a 128-bit value with no branches.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit layout via hi/lo; byte-level reversal; pure bit tricks.
- 64-bit byte swap implementation using shifts/masks.
- Branchless data movement across two halves.

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t bswap64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32) | ((x & 0xFFFFFFFF00000000ULL) >> 32);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x & 0xFFFF0000FFFF0000ULL) >> 16);
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8)  | ((x & 0xFF00FF00FF00FF00ULL) >> 8);
  return x;
}
void swapEndianness128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo){
  *out_hi = bswap64(lo);
  *out_lo = bswap64(hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to a 256-bit type? (Explain any performance considerations.)
- Could you implement a generic macro to swap endianness for any multiple of 8 bytes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to manipulate a 128-bit value by composing 64-bit operations, enforcing a non-loop, fixed-sequence approach for performance.

## Key Concepts
- Endianness and byte-level reversal
- Bit-twiddling for 64-bit reversal
- Combining two 64-bit halves into a 128-bit result

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t v) {
  v = ((v & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((v >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  v = ((v & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((v >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  v = (v << 32) | (v >> 32);
  return v;
}
uint128 swapEndian128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
  r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if only a single 128-bit type isn’t available?
- How would you validate correctness with representative test patterns (all zeros, all ones, alternating bits)?

## Why This Is Asked
Endianness matters across 128-bit data paths; swapping bytes is a frequent low-level operation in serialization and network code.

## Key Concepts
- Endianness and cross-word byte reversal
- SWAR bit-twiddling for bswap64
- Handling 128-bit via hi/lo without 128-bit types

## Code Example
```c
static uint64_t swap64(uint64_t x) {
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  x = (x << 32) | (x >> 32);
  return x;
}
void swapEndian128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  *out_hi = swap64(lo);
  *out_lo = swap64(hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you generalize to 256-bit values?
- How would you test correctness with random inputs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 128-bit data as four independent 32-bit lanes and perform lane-wise rotation without loops. Demonstrates precise bit-slicing, correct boundary handling, and minimal, provable code paths.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit packing into 4×32 lanes
- Lane-wise rotation using (x<<n) | (x>>(32-n))
- Bit masking to confine intermediate results to 32-bit lanes
- Clamping n with & 31 to handle n >= 32

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64 lo; uint64 hi; } uint128;

uint128 rotateWithinLanes128(uint128 x, unsigned n) {
    n &= 31;
    uint32 a = (uint32)(x.lo);
    uint32 b = (uint32)(x.lo >> 32);
    uint32 c = (uint32)(x.hi);
    uint32 d = (uint32)(x.hi >> 32);

    x.lo = ((uint64)((a << n) | (a >> (32 - n))) << 32) |
           ((b << n) | (b >> (32 - n)));
    x.hi = ((uint64)((c << n) | (c >> (32 - n))) << 32) |
           ((d << n) | (d >> (32 - n)));
    return x;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests compact, deterministic bit-trickery without loops, showing knowledge of parallel popcount progressions.

## Key Concepts
- Masks for pairwise sums
- Shifts to propagate counts
- Edge-case: 0 yields 0

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t popcount32(uint32_t x) {
  x = (x & 0x55555555) + ((x >> 1) & 0x55555555);
  x = (x & 0x33333333) + ((x >> 2) & 0x33333333);
  x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F) + ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F);
  x = (x & 0x00FF00FF) + ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF);
  x = (x & 0x0000FFFF) + ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF);
  return x & 0x3F;
}
```

